AlgoTrade: SQLite -> PostgreSQL on AWS RDS
demo pair: data/demo/20261117_1435/trading_bot.db + Postgres database cloudsync_demo_20261117_1435; prepared run artifacts/demo/20261117-1435

==============================================================================
  3. Integrity constraints and the quarantine   (~3 min)
  Syllabus topic: CHECK, NOT NULL, FK; rejected by the database
==============================================================================
  SAY:
   - Postgres decides what's invalid, not Python (D11). Python only routes the failure.
   - A rejected row is kept in rejected_rows with the constraint it broke, so nothing is silently lost.

  > Insert an impossible candle (high 90, low 110) straight into Postgres
  PASS  rejected by Postgres: new row for relation "candles" violates check constraint "candles_ohlc_close_in_range"

  > What the bulk load quarantined (rejected_rows)
    source table  constraint            rows
    ------------  --------------------  ----
    delta_cache   delta_snapshots_pkey  2050
    delta_cache: 2,050 duplicate groups on the natural key. The highest source id wins (D10),
    the rest are kept here instead of being dropped.
  [Enter = next step]

  > Now the bot writes a bad candle (close above high) into SQLite, and we sync
    SQLite accepted close = 22878.45 for candle id 28148126 (high is 22828.45). SQLite has no CHECK here.
    $ cloudsync sync once --sqlite data/demo/20261117_1435/trading_bot.db --dsn "<demo dsn as sync_writer>"
    |   "upserts": 0,
    |   "deletes": 0,
    |   "rejected": 1,
    |   "paused_tables": [],
    |   "backlog": 0
    | }
    exit 0 in 1.2 s
  PASS  the cloud quarantined it: [('28148126', 'candles_ohlc_close_in_range')]
  [Enter = next step]

  > The bot corrects the row, and we sync again
    $ cloudsync sync once --sqlite data/demo/20261117_1435/trading_bot.db --dsn "<demo dsn as sync_writer>"
    |   "upserts": 1,
    |   "deletes": 0,
    |   "rejected": 0,
    |   "paused_tables": [],
    |   "backlog": 0
    | }
    exit 0 in 1.1 s
  PASS  corrected row left the quarantine and is back in the cloud: (datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 27, 15, 29), 22824.85, 22828.45, 22820.55, 22824.7)
  [Enter = next step]

==============================================================================
  4. Change capture and crash safety   (~4 min)
  Syllabus topic: triggers, transactions (atomicity), recovery, idempotence
==============================================================================
  SAY:
   - AFTER INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE triggers in SQLite log (table, pk, op, seq) into _changelog.
   - The sync reads a batch, applies it AND moves the cursor in ONE Postgres transaction.
   - So a crash leaves the cloud fully before or fully after a batch. Never half.

  > The bot updates candle 28148125 in SQLite (close 22825.1 -> 22812.1)
    The trigger captured it in _changelog:
    seq     table           pk        op
    ------  --------------  --------  --
    691012  market_data_1m  28148125  U 
    Cloud before sync: (datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 27, 15, 28), 22818.65, 22829.6, 22812.1, 22825.1)

  > sync once
    $ cloudsync sync once --sqlite data/demo/20261117_1435/trading_bot.db --dsn "<demo dsn as sync_writer>"
    |   "upserts": 1,
    |   "deletes": 0,
    |   "rejected": 0,
    |   "paused_tables": [],
    |   "backlog": 0
    | }
    exit 0 in 1.0 s
  PASS  cloud after sync: (datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 27, 15, 28), 22818.65, 22829.6, 22812.1, 22812.1)
    _changelog entries left for market_data_1m: 0 (pruned after Postgres acknowledged)
  [Enter = next step]

  > Now crash the sync in the middle of a batch (candle 28148125: close -> 22829.6)
    $ cloudsync sync once --sqlite data/demo/20261117_1435/trading_bot.db --dsn "<demo dsn as sync_writer>"
      (with CLOUDSYNC_FAULT=mid_batch:1)
    | [23:21:38] FAULT INJECTED: dying at mid_batch of batch 1
    exit 75 in 0.9 s
  PASS  the sync process died (exit 75, like kill -9)
  PASS  cloud unchanged: close = 22812.1 (still 22812.1); cursor last_seq 691012 -> 691012
    The change is still waiting in SQLite: 1 entry, nothing lost.
  [Enter = next step]

  > Restart the sync: it resumes from the cursor
    $ cloudsync sync once --sqlite data/demo/20261117_1435/trading_bot.db --dsn "<demo dsn as sync_writer>"
    |   "upserts": 1,
    |   "deletes": 0,
    |   "rejected": 0,
    |   "paused_tables": [],
    |   "backlog": 0
    | }
    exit 0 in 0.9 s
  PASS  cloud now: close = 22829.6; applied exactly once
    $ cloudsync sync once --sqlite data/demo/20261117_1435/trading_bot.db --dsn "<demo dsn as sync_writer>"
    |   "upserts": 1,
    |   "deletes": 0,
    |   "rejected": 0,
    |   "paused_tables": [],
    |   "backlog": 0
    | }
    exit 0 in 0.9 s
    (candle 28148125 restored to close = 22825.1 and synced)
